/**
@page exa_time_dg_cantilever_sensitivity Time DG cantilever adjoint sensitivity verification

The sensitivity input exercises report.pdf's DG(1), alpha=4 scheme.
The input selects linear plane-strain elasticity or finite-deformation StVK through
gDGNonlinear. The domain is the cantilever [0,3]x[0,h], clamped at x=0.
A horizontal GEN level set y-h=0 cuts the upper surface out of a [0,3]x[0,0.8]
background mesh with 12x4 elements, Q2 B-splines and conformal XTK integration.
Phase 0 is the only active material. mu=3, lambda=5 correspond to E=7.875,
nu=0.3125. Reference density is one. The clamp uses symmetric Nitsche and
cut-cell stabilization uses ghost-normal-field terms; both receive the same
reduced weak/collocation time treatment as bulk forces.

@section exa_time_dg_sensitivity Adjoint sensitivity check

Time_DG_Cantilever_Sensitivity starts at rest and applies the reference-volume
body load (0,-a*sin(pi*t/T)), a=0.15. The objective is integral(u_y) at T, and
the constraint/second criterion is final beam volume. There are exactly two
scalar ADVs: level-set center_y=h and the constant GEN DENSITY property.
The density changes reference mass only, with stiffness and applied force held
fixed. The height moves the cut boundary and therefore changes integration,
shape functions evaluated on cut cells, and both mass and stiffness.

The SWEEP algorithm records adjoint gradients and forward/backward/central
finite differences at eps=1e-5 and 5e-6. The checker requires finite, nonzero
objective gradients for both ADVs and agreement within 1 percent plus an
absolute tolerance of 1e-7. The volume derivatives provide independent checks:
dV/dh=3, dV/drho=0. Both nonlinear and linear cases use identical meshes,
loads, time slabs and solver tolerances. The adjoint differentiates the two
preceding displacement slab dependencies of reconstructed velocity.


The companion @ref exa_time_dg_cantilever forward example remains under
projects/EXA/structure/dynamics/Time_DG_Cantilever and checks analytical fields.

The input is a self-contained .cpp file with direct MORIS includes. The shared
EXA main initializes MORIS/Catch, and each CTest case has its own working
directory. Driver globals select linear or nonlinear elasticity; standalone
input builds supply their own defaults. No environment-variable configuration
or PETSC_OPTIONS is required.

@section exa_time_dg_sensitivity_commands Build and execution
@verbatim
cmake --build build_dbg --target Main_Time_DG_Cantilever_Sensitivity --parallel 4
ctest --test-dir build_dbg -R '^Time_DG_Cantilever-Sensitivity' --output-on-failure
@endverbatim

The supported adjoint objectives depend on displacement and volume.
Design-dependent initial fields, reconstructed-velocity objectives, moving
meshes and rate-dependent materials require additional derivative terms.
Unit tests tagged [TimeDG] cover tangents, history and delayed adjoint coupling.
*/
